External surfaces on Linux (GPUI patch 1 of docs/plans/20261014-gpui-frontend.md, the scene and window half; the
renderer half is build/patches/gpui-pre-wgpu). `PaintSurface` gains a Linux payload, `ExternalSurface`: a dmabuf frame
the renderer imports (no CPU copy: acquired from the producer's foreign queue family and copied on the GPU) or a
CPU-pixel frame it uploads by damaged rectangle, each reporting once how it ended (`FrameOutcome::Presented` only after
the GPU finished with it, `Lost` when that cannot be known). `Window::paint_external_surface` paints one, as macOS's
`paint_surface` paints a CVPixelBuffer. `dmabuf_import_caps` publishes what the renderer's device imports (its DRM
render node and the formats and modifiers it takes), for a producer to allocate on that device;
`external_surface_stats` counts imports, copies and uploads. An upstream candidate (Zed): the Linux counterpart of the
macOS surface primitive. scripts/vendor-gpui.sh applies this to the verified crates.io archive.

